    "finish_elapsed_s": 11.517869992996566,
    "text": "A refrigerator works by moving heat, not by making \"cold.\" A special liquid called refrigerant flows through pipes. First, it evaporates inside the fridge, soaking up heat from your food—like sweat cooling your skin. That warm gas then gets squeezed by a compressor, which makes it hot. Next, it flows through coils behind or under the fridge, where the heat escapes into your kitchen. Now cooler, the refrigerant turns back into a liquid and repeats the cycle. A thermostat watches the temperature and switches the compressor on and off. Insulation in the walls helps trap the cold inside, so your milk stays chilly!"
  }
